sysl.regex — reading a pattern
==============================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
    import sysl.text.{Ascii, Chars, chars_of}

A recursive-descent parser for POSIX Extended Regular Expressions. The grammar it implements, which
is POSIX's own with the recursion arranged for descent:

```
regex   → branch ('|' branch)*
branch  → piece*
piece   → atom quantifier*
atom    → '.' | '^' | '$' | '[' set ']' | '(' regex ')' | '\' char | ordinary
quantif → '*' | '+' | '?' | '{' n '}' | '{' n ',' '}' | '{' n ',' m '}'
set     → '^'? ']'? item* ']'
item    → char '-' char | '[:' name ':]' | char
```

A branch may be empty, which is what makes `a|` and `()` legal, and is why `branch` is `piece*`
rather than `piece+`.


Escaping, and what is deliberately not supported
------------------------------------------------

In ERE the metacharacters are unescaped and a backslash makes the next character ordinary: `(` opens
a group and `\(` is a left parenthesis, which is the opposite of Basic Regular Expressions. Only ERE
is implemented. BRE exists for `expr` and `sed`, and nothing here has asked for it.

**A backslash inside a bracket expression is an ordinary character**, as POSIX requires. So `[\t]`
is the two characters backslash and `t`, not a tab, and a `]` is included by writing it first:
`[]abc]`. This is the one place where the strict reading is likely to surprise, and it is strict on
purpose — the alternative silently gives a different set than the same pattern gives to `grep -E`.

**`{` always begins an interval.** A pattern wanting a literal brace writes `\{`.


The cursor
----------

Position is kept by a `Chars` cursor from `sysl.text` rather than by an index into the bytes, which
is what lets the parser handle a pattern containing text: `[é-ü]` is a range over two characters,
and a parser stepping bytes would see four. The cursor reports the byte offset of the character it
is about to read, so every error position below is directly an offset into the pattern string.

A `Chars` is a value, so a copy of one is a second cursor — which is the whole of the two-character
lookahead that a range inside a bracket expression needs.

    private struct Parser
        cur: Chars
        groups: int

        at(self) -> usize = self.cur.offset

        peek(self) -> Option[char] = self.cur.peek()

        done(self) -> bool = self.cur.peek().is_none()

        bump(*self)
            self.cur.next()

The character after the one the cursor is on, without moving: a second cursor, made by copying the
first and stepping it once.

        peek2(self) -> Option[char]
            var look = self.cur

            look.next()
            look.peek()
        end peek2

Whether the cursor is on exactly this character, and the step-past that every caller closing a
bracket, a group or an interval turns into its error.

        on(self, c: char) -> bool = self.cur.peek() match
            Some(x) -> x == c
            None -> false

        on2(self, c: char) -> bool = self.peek2() match
            Some(x) -> x == c
            None -> false

        eat(*self, c: char) -> bool
            if self.on(c)
                self.bump()
                return true

            false
        end eat

Alternation is the loosest operator, so it is the outermost rule. It is left-associated, which for
`a|b|c` gives `alt(alt(a, b), c)` — the shape does not matter to the matcher, since both branches of
an `Alt` are tried, but it is fixed here so the tests can say what it is.

        parse_alt(*self) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var left = self.parse_branch()?

            while self.on('|')
                self.bump()

                var right = self.parse_branch()?

                left = Alt(left, right)

            Ok(left)
        end parse_alt

A branch runs until something that cannot start an atom: the end of the pattern, a `|`, or a `)`.
Anything else is a piece, and the pieces are concatenated left to right.

        parse_branch(*self) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var left: &Node = Empty
            var any = false

            while !self.done() && !self.on('|') && !self.on(')')
                var piece = self.parse_piece()?

                left = if any then Seq(left, piece) else piece
                any = true

            Ok(left)
        end parse_branch

Quantifiers are looped rather than taken singly, so `a*?` is `quest(star(a))` — the reading every
ERE implementation gives it, and the reason it is not laziness: ERE has no lazy quantifiers, and the
second `?` makes the already-optional star optional again.

        parse_piece(*self) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var node = self.parse_atom()?
            var more = true

            while more && !self.done()
                var here = self.at()

                if self.eat('*') then
                    node = Star(node)
                elif self.eat('+') then
                    node = Plus(node)
                elif self.eat('?') then
                    node = Quest(node)
                elif self.eat('{') then
                    node = self.parse_interval(node, here)?
                else
                    more = false

            Ok(node)
        end parse_piece

        parse_atom(*self) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var here = self.at()

            self.peek() match
                None -> Err(NothingToRepeat(here))
                Some(c) ->
                    if c == '.' then
                        self.bump()
                        Ok(AnyChar)
                    elif c == '^' then
                        self.bump()
                        Ok(AtStart)
                    elif c == '$' then
                        self.bump()
                        Ok(AtEnd)
                    elif c == '[' then
                        self.bump()
                        self.parse_set(here)
                    elif c == '(' then
                        self.bump()
                        self.parse_group(here)
                    elif c == '\\' then
                        self.bump()
                        self.parse_escape(here)
                    elif c == '*' || c == '+' || c == '?' || c == '{' then
                        Err(NothingToRepeat(here))
                    else
                        self.bump()
                        Ok(Lit(c))
        end parse_atom

        parse_escape(*self, start: usize) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            self.peek() match
                None -> Err(TrailingBackslash(start))
                Some(e) ->
                    self.bump()
                    Ok(Lit(e))
        end parse_escape

Groups are numbered in the order their `(` is read, which is what makes the numbering match the way
a pattern reads left to right even when the groups nest. The counter is bumped before the body is
parsed for that reason: `((a)b)` gives the outer group 1 and the inner group 2.

        parse_group(*self, open_at: usize) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            self.groups += 1

            var index = self.groups
            var inner = self.parse_alt()?

            if !self.eat(')') then return Err(UnterminatedGroup(open_at))
            Ok(Group(inner, index))
        end parse_group

`a{2,4}` is not a node kind. It is expanded into the nodes that already exist, which keeps the
matcher's instruction set at nine and means nothing downstream has to know intervals happened. The
expansion itself is `expand`, below.

        parse_interval(*self, child: &Node, open_at: usize) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var lo = self.parse_count()

            if lo < 0 then return Err(MissingIntervalCount(open_at))

            var hi = lo

            if self.eat(',')
                if self.on('}') then
                    hi = -1
                else
                    hi = self.parse_count()
                    if hi < 0 then return Err(MissingIntervalCount(open_at))

            if !self.eat('}') then return Err(UnterminatedInterval(open_at))
            if hi >= 0 && hi < lo then return Err(BackwardsInterval(open_at))

            Ok(expand(child, lo, hi))
        end parse_interval

        parse_count(*self) -> int
            var n = 0
            var any = false

            while !self.done()
                var c = self.peek().unwrap()

                if !c.is_digit() then break
                n = n * 10 + int(u32(c)) - 48
                any = true
                self.bump()

            if any then n else -1
        end parse_count

The two quirks POSIX puts at the front of a bracket expression are both about a character that would
otherwise close or negate it. A `^` first negates; anywhere else it is ordinary. A `]` first is an
ordinary `]`; anywhere else it closes. Both fall out of reading the `^` before the loop and of the
loop's `first` flag.

        parse_set(*self, open_at: usize) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
            var cls = char_class()

            if self.eat('^') then cls.negated = true

            var first = true

            loop
                if self.done() then return Err(UnterminatedClass(open_at))
                if self.on(']') && !first then break

                first = false
                self.parse_set_item(cls)?

            self.bump()
            Ok(InSet(cls))
        end parse_set

One member of a class: a named class, a range, or a single character. A `-` counts as a range only
when there is something on both sides of it, so a trailing `[a-]` is the two ordinary characters `a`
and `-`, which is again what POSIX says.

        parse_set_item(*self, cls: CharClass) -> Result[unit, RegexError]
            var here = self.at()

            if self.on('[') && self.on2(':')
                self.bump()
                self.bump()
                return self.parse_class_name(cls, here)

            var lo = self.peek().unwrap()

            self.bump()

            if self.on('-') && self.peek2().is_some() && !self.on2(']')
                self.bump()

                var hi = self.peek().unwrap()

                self.bump()

                if u32(hi) < u32(lo) then return Err(BackwardsRange(here))
                cls.ranges.push(CharRange(u32(lo), u32(hi)))
                return Ok(())

            cls.ranges.push(CharRange(u32(lo), u32(lo)))
            Ok(())
        end parse_set_item

The name between `[:` and `:]`. An unknown one is refused rather than treated as ordinary
characters: `[[:alhpa:]]` is a typo every time, and a class that quietly stood for the letters of
"alhpa" plus a colon would match and give a wrong answer instead of an error.

        parse_class_name(*self, cls: CharClass, open_at: usize) -> Result[unit, RegexError]
            var name = ""

            while !self.done() && !self.on(':')
                name += str(self.peek().unwrap())
                self.bump()

            if !self.eat(':') then return Err(UnterminatedClassName(open_at))
            if !self.eat(']') then return Err(UnterminatedClassName(open_at))

            named_class(name) match
                Some(n) ->
                    cls.names.push(n)
                    Ok(())
                None -> Err(UnknownClassName(open_at, name))
        end parse_class_name
    end Parser


Expanding an interval
---------------------

An interval becomes the nodes that already exist:

```
a{3}    →  seq(seq(a, a), a)
a{2,4}  →  seq(seq(a, a), seq(quest(a), quest(a)))
a{3,}   →  seq(seq(seq(a, a), a), star(a))
a{0,2}  →  seq(quest(a), quest(a))
a{0}    →  empty
```

The child subtree is *shared* between the copies rather than duplicated. That is sound because
nothing mutates a parsed tree, and the compiler emits an independent run of instructions for each
place it walks to. A capture group inside a repeated piece therefore has one number and is written
by every repetition that runs, so the group holds what the last one matched — which is what POSIX
says it should.

    private expand(child: &Node, lo: int, hi: int) -> &Node
        var out: &Node = Empty
        var any = false

        for _ in 0..<lo
            out = if any then Seq(out, child) else child
            any = true

        if hi < 0 then
            var tail: &Node = Star(child)

            out = if any then Seq(out, tail) else tail
        else
            for _ in 0..<(hi - lo)
                var opt: &Node = Quest(child)

                out = if any then Seq(out, opt) else opt
                any = true

        out
    end expand


The entry points
----------------

A pattern is a whole regex followed by nothing. The leftover-input case is reported as an unmatched
`)` because that is the only thing it can be: a branch stops at `)` whether or not a group is open,
so an unbalanced one is exactly what is left behind.

    parse(pattern: string) -> Result[&Node, RegexError]
        var p = Parser(chars_of(pattern.bytes), 0)
        var n = p.parse_alt()?

        if !p.done() then return Err(UnmatchedParen(p.at()))
        Ok(n)
    end parse

How many capture groups a pattern has, which the compiler needs before it can size the capture
slots. It is returned alongside the tree rather than counted by walking it afterwards, because the
parser has already counted and a second walk is a second chance for the two to disagree.

    parse_counted(pattern: string) -> Result[(&Node, int), RegexError]
        var p = Parser(chars_of(pattern.bytes), 0)
        var n = p.parse_alt()?

        if !p.done() then return Err(UnmatchedParen(p.at()))
        Ok((n, p.groups))
    end parse_counted
